# 5. RESUME VERSION CONTROL SYSTEM

This is a critical feature.

Every published resume must have a permanent version number.

Initial version:

v1.0 — Original Resume.

Example future versions:

v1.1 — Updated Skills and Certification.

v1.2 — New Professional Experience.

v1.3 — New Projects.

v2.0 — Major Resume Structure or Career Profile Update.

Use semantic versioning rules:

PATCH:
Minor corrections such as spelling, formatting or non-substantive wording.

MINOR:
New skills, projects, certifications, education or experience entries.

MAJOR:
Significant professional profile restructuring or a major resume template change.

The administrator may also manually assign a version, but duplicate versions must not be allowed.

## Version history collection

Create a resumeVersions MongoDB collection.

Fields:

- version.
- title.
- description.
- releaseNotes.
- generatedAt.
- publishedAt.
- createdBy.
- status.
- profileSnapshot.
- contentHash.
- pdfUrl.
- docxUrl.
- templateVersion.
- isLatest.
- isPublic.
- changeSummary.

Each version must store an immutable snapshot of the professional profile used to generate it.

Store its generated PDF and DOCX in persistent storage.

Never regenerate an older version using newer profile information.

Never overwrite an already published historical document.

A new update must create a new version.

Provide rollback functionality by creating a new version from a previous snapshot. Do not silently mutate historical releases.

